WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.080 --> 00:00:06.160
In today's Business English episode we’re&nbsp;
talking about the art of negotiation.

00:00:06.160 --> 00:00:10.240
Now, negotiating looks&nbsp;
different in different cultures.

00:00:10.240 --> 00:00:14.080
But, today we’re going to&nbsp;
look at the first two steps&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:14.080 --> 00:00:19.440
and language must-haves to negotiate&nbsp;
within a British cultural context.

00:00:19.440 --> 00:00:23.520
So, to start off with, you&nbsp;
have to take step number 1:

00:00:23.520 --> 00:00:26.800
Learn the gentle art of small talk.

00:00:26.800 --> 00:00:29.840
Don’t get down to business straight away.

00:00:29.840 --> 00:00:33.760
Morning, morning, so I’ll give&nbsp;
you £200 for 10 garden gomes.

00:00:34.480 --> 00:00:37.200
That approach is just going to frighten people,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:37.200 --> 00:00:42.320
and make everyone feel uncomfortable.
Rather, you need to break the ice.

00:00:43.360 --> 00:00:47.360
Hi, lovely day today isn’t it?&nbsp;
I thought it was going to rain.&nbsp;

00:00:47.360 --> 00:00:51.680
I know, we should conduct this meeting&nbsp;
outside, although apparently it’s not&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:51.680 --> 00:00:56.640
going to last. My app says it’s going&nbsp;
to rain in 10 minutes and 20 seconds.

00:00:56.640 --> 00:00:58.240
Or

00:00:58.240 --> 00:01:01.840
Hi
Hello, welcome! Before we start,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:01.840 --> 00:01:08.160
would you like a cup of tea and some cake?
Yes please, oh my goodness, you’ve read my mind!&nbsp;

00:01:08.160 --> 00:01:13.440
This cake is just amazing, did&nbsp;
you, did you make it this morning?&nbsp;

00:01:13.440 --> 00:01:18.560
Oh that’s very kind of you, no, no, no, it’s just&nbsp;
Marks and Spencers, but they’re good aren’t they?

00:01:18.560 --> 00:01:21.280
The best small talk is on topics that aren’t&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:21.280 --> 00:01:26.800
controversial. Never talk about things&nbsp;
that people feel too emotional about,

00:01:26.800 --> 00:01:33.600
for example, politics, finances,&nbsp;
whether you love or hate Marmite.

00:01:33.600 --> 00:01:39.600
The best and most comfortable small talk&nbsp;
is when you talk about everyday things.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:39.600 --> 00:01:42.080
Food, travel, the weather.

00:01:42.080 --> 00:01:48.400
Once you’ve covered the appropriate subjects for&nbsp;
small talk, you need to take step number two:

00:01:48.400 --> 00:01:54.000
Open negotiations reluctantly.
I’m sorry, you’re not there to negotiate, they're&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:54.000 --> 00:01:57.520
not there to negotiate, who’s here to negotiate?
You all are.&nbsp;

00:01:57.520 --> 00:02:03.760
After 10 minutes of tea, cake, talking about&nbsp;
whether you should bring your umbrella to work&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:03.760 --> 00:02:08.160
tomorrow, you can shift into the,&nbsp;
'let’s get down to business' gear.

00:02:08.160 --> 00:02:12.640
Do me a favour, take a pen and paper&nbsp;
and write down these sentences.

00:02:13.280 --> 00:02:17.280
'I suppose we’d better get&nbsp;
started. Does anyone mind?'&nbsp;

00:02:17.280 --> 00:02:20.880
'If no one objects, shall we&nbsp;
take a look at the agenda?'&nbsp;

00:02:20.880 --> 00:02:24.480
'Would anyone like more tea&nbsp;
before we get cracking?'

00:02:24.480 --> 00:02:28.640
Remember, you need to make it sound&nbsp;
like you’re having such a good time&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:28.640 --> 00:02:32.320
and the last thing you want&nbsp;
to do is start negotiations.

00:02:32.320 --> 00:02:34.480
Cue step number three:

00:02:34.480 --> 00:02:35.920
Make your proposal.

00:02:35.920 --> 00:02:38.320
But I won’t talk about it in this video.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:38.320 --> 00:02:43.120
If you want to read about the 6 language&nbsp;
must-haves to negotiate in English,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:43.120 --> 00:02:49.840
